{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012nHvKwh8t4c8JNRGScVUxK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Implementation translation guide (Phase 4)\n2\t\n3\tA focused reference for filling in `todo!()` bodies in\n4\t`rs/fq/src/`.  Companion to `xlate/test_translation_guide.md`\n5\t(which covers the test side); they share the API surface\n6\toverview and naming conventions, but this guide focuses on\n7\t*body* translation.\n8\t\n9\t**Read this first.**\n10\t\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\t## NO SHORTCUTS — the prime directive\n14\t\n15\tPhase 4's job is to **translate the entire library**, not to fill\n16\tthe easy bodies and stub the hard ones.  Past sweeps have produced\n17\thundreds of \"completed\" stubs that returned `None` / `Err(Generic)`\n18\t/ `Ok(())` plus a `// SKIP:` comment.  **That is failure, not\n19\tcompletion.**  The gate now flags `todo!()`, `unimplemented!()`,\n20\tand `// SKIP:` markers all the same.\n21\t\n22\tIf a body is hard — borrow checker, lifetimes, missing API\n23\tsurface, missing struct fields — **do the hard work**:\n24\t\n25\t* If the borrow checker says `&mut` from `&self`, use the actual\n26\t  Rust idiom (`&mut self`, `RefCell`, restructure the call site).\n27\t  Don't return `None` and call it done.\n28\t* If a method needs a static table the C side has, **port the\n29\t  table** to a Rust `static` / `const`.  Don't fabricate a\n30\t  placeholder.\n31\t* If the Rust API is missing a field a C body uses, **add the\n32\t  field** to the Rust struct.  Don't skip the function.\n33\t* If the Rust API is missing a helper a C body needs, **translate\n34\t  the helper first**, then come back.\n35\t\n36\tThe only legitimate way to leave a body unfinished is `todo!()`\n37\twith a clear blocker note above it explaining what it depends on.\n38\t**Never `unimplemented!()`, never `// SKIP:`, never a fake stub\n39\treturn** — those hide the work behind the gate.\n40\t\n41\t---\n42\t\n43\t## Operating mandate\n44\t\n45\t* **Translate every C body faithfully.**  Walk `picoquic/<src>.c`\n46\t  for each function whose Rust counterpart is incomplete and write\n47\t  the equivalent Rust body.  Don't sidestep with a simpler-looking\n48\t  algorithm — the C body is the spec.\n49\t* **Tests are the gate.**  After each meaningful chunk, run\n50\t  `cd rs/fq && cargo test` and watch the panic count drop.  A\n51\t  function is \"done\" when the tests that reach it stop panicking.\n52\t* **Idiomatic Rust over C-mirroring.**  Phase 1 already shaped\n53\t  signatures.  Phase 4 is where the shape pays off — use Rust\n54\t  idioms in the body even when they don't match the C control\n55\t  flow.\n56\t* **No `unsafe`.**  No edits outside `rs/fq/src/`.\n57\t\n58\t---\n59\t\n60\t## C body patterns → Rust idioms\n61\t\n62\t### Memory and ownership\n63\t\n64\t| C pattern | Rust translation |\n65\t|---|---|\n66\t| `T* p = malloc(sizeof(T)); ...; free(p);` | `let p = Box::new(T { ... });` (auto `Drop`) |\n67\t| `T arr[N]` on stack, returns by ptr | `[T; N]` or `&[T; N]`, never `*const T` |\n68\t| `(uint8_t* buf, size_t len)` aliasing call | `&[u8]` (read) / `&mut [u8]` (write); slice carries len |\n69\t| Linked list: `T* head; T* cur->next` | `Vec<T>` or `VecDeque<T>` if FIFO; iterate with `.iter()` |\n70\t| Intrusive node: `struct T { …; next *T; }` | Token-based: parent owns `Vec<T>`, callers hold an [`Arena`]/[`SplayTree`]/[`HashTable`] token |\n71\t| `void* opaque_handle` | `Box<dyn Trait>` (one trait per shape) |\n72\t| `static T global` | `static T` if `T: Sync` else `pub fn name() -> T` accessor |\n73\t\n74\t### Control flow\n75\t\n76\t| C pattern | Rust translation |\n77\t|---|---|\n78\t| `if (ret == 0) { ... } else { ret = -1; }` | `if x { … }`; propagate fallibility with `?` |\n79\t| `goto cleanup; cleanup: free(p); return ret;` | Rust `Drop` runs automatically; `?` for early-return |\n80\t| `int ret = 0; ... ret \\|= f(...); ... return ret;` | Compose `Result` with `?`; never use bitwise-OR for status |\n81\t| `for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) { ... }` | `for i in 0..n { ... }` or `.iter().enumerate()` |\n82\t| `while (cur != NULL) { ... cur = cur->next; }` | `while let Some(node) = ...` or `.iter()` |\n83\t| `switch (e) { case X: ...; case Y: ...; default: ...; }` | `match e { X => ..., Y => ..., _ => ... }`, exhaustive |\n84\t| `assert(cond)` | `assert!(cond)` or `debug_assert!(cond)` |\n85\t\n86\t### Errors and returns\n87\t\n88\t| C pattern | Rust translation |\n89\t|---|---|\n90\t| `int` 0/-1 status | `Result<(), Error>`; map `0 → Ok(())`, `-1 → Err(Error::…)` |\n91\t| `int` 0/PICOQUIC_ERROR_X | `Result<T, Error>`; map error codes to specific `Error::…` variants |\n92\t| Out-parameter `T* result` | `-> Result<T, Error>` returning the value by-move |\n93\t| `size_t` returning bytes-written, with `SIZE_MAX` on failure | `Result<usize, Error>`, never `usize::MAX` as a sentinel |\n94\t| `bool` returning 0/1 | `bool` |\n95\t\n96\t### Strings and bytes\n97\t\n98\t| C pattern | Rust translation |\n99\t|---|---|\n100\t| `char const*` (read-only string) | `&str` |\n101\t| `char*` (owned) | `String` |\n102\t| `(char* str, size_t cap)` (caller-allocated) | `&mut [u8]` or `&mut String`; document encoding |\n103\t| `memcpy(dst, src, n)` | `dst.copy_from_slice(src)` (if same len) |\n104\t| `memset(buf, c, n)` | `buf.fill(c)` |\n105\t| `memcmp(a, b, n)` | `a == b` (slice-equality) or `a.cmp(b)` |\n106\t| `picoquic_constant_time_memcmp` | [`crate::utils::constant_time_memcmp`] |\n107\t\n108\t### Time\n109\t\n110\t* `uint64_t current_time` (microseconds) → [`crate::Instant`] (`fugit::Instant<u64, 1, 1_000_000>`).\n111\t* Duration / RTT / interval → [`crate::Duration`].\n112\t* Construct with `Instant::from_ticks(usec)` / `Duration::from_ticks(usec)`.\n113\t* Subtract instants: `now - earlier` returns `Duration`.\n114\t\n115\t### Network\n116\t\n117\t* `struct sockaddr*` → `&core::net::SocketAddr`.\n118\t* IP address stored as bytes → `core::net::IpAddr`.\n119\t* Port byte-order: keep host-order in Rust; do `.to_be_bytes()` /\n120\t  `.from_be_bytes` only at the wire boundary.\n121\t\n122\t### Logging / formatting\n123\t\n124\t* `printf` / `fprintf` / `picoquic_log_xxx` → `log::{trace, debug,\n125\t  info, warn, error}` macros where appropriate; or write into a\n126\t  `&mut impl core::fmt::Write` buffer for the existing logger\n127\t  callbacks.\n128\t* `va_list` / variadic → `core::fmt::Arguments<'_>`; the call site\n129\t  uses `format_args!`.\n130\t* `snprintf` / `sprintf` → `format!` / `write!` / `core::fmt::Write`.\n131\t* Hex / binary formatting: use `{:02x}`, `{:08b}`, etc.\n132\t\n133\t### Crypto\n134\t\n135\t* AES / ChaCha20: use the Rust Crypto trait crates (`aead`,\n136\t  `cipher`, `digest`) and concrete impls (`aes`, `chacha20`).\n137\t* Header protection lives in [`crate::header_protection`]; AEAD\n138\t  packet-key plumbing in [`crate::tls::PacketKey`] (impls in\n139\t  [`crate::sys::picotls`] / [`crate::sys::openssl`] backends).\n140\t* HKDF with SHA-256 for QUIC initial secrets (RFC 9001 fixes\n141\t  this); use the `hkdf` crate.\n142\t\n143\t---\n144\t\n145\t## Anti-patterns — these are forbidden, period\n146\t\n147\tThe gate detects all of these.  None of them is \"Phase 4 done\":\n148\t\n149\t* **`unimplemented!()`** — same runtime effect as `todo!()` but\n150\t  often used by agents to feel productive while skipping work.\n151\t  Treat it as identical to `todo!()`.\n152\t* **`// SKIP:` markers with stub returns** — `None` / `Err(Generic)`\n153\t  / `Ok(())` plus a comment explaining why you couldn't.  This is\n154\t  the worst pattern: it compiles, it doesn't panic obnoxiously,\n155\t  it looks like a translation, and it silently breaks every\n156\t  caller.  **Do not write `// SKIP:` comments.**\n157\t* **`Err(Error::Generic)` stubs** — same as `// SKIP:` without the\n158\t  honesty.  Forbidden.\n159\t* **`Ok(())` no-ops for functions with real C-side side effects** —\n160\t  running scenarios, building state, walking qlog files, mutating\n161\t  shared state.  Forbidden.\n162\t* **Fabricated return values** — returning `0` / `false` / `None`\n163\t  / a placeholder default when the C body computes something real.\n164\t  Forbidden.\n165\t* **\"Half-translated\" bodies** that skip the C function's harder\n166\t  branches (e.g. translating the success path and writing\n167\t  `todo!()` for the error path).  Forbidden.\n168\t\n169\t### Nothing is fundamentally untranslatable\n170\t\n171\tEvery function in picoquic has a body.  Translate it.  The C\n172\tbody is the spec; produce equivalent Rust.  Phase 4 IS the\n173\ttranslation — it covers the whole library, not \"the easy parts.\"\n174\t\n175\t* **`pthread_create` / thread-spawning** → `std::thread::spawn`.\n176\t  Rust has full threading support.  picoquic spawns network\n177\t  threads internally; translate that with `std::thread` and\n178\t  `std::sync::mpsc`.  The fact that the public `Quic` type is\n179\t  not `Send`/`Sync` (caller serializes access) is a Rust-API\n180\t  property; it does **not** excuse skipping threading code in\n181\t  the C body.\n182\t* **Cross-thread wake-up** (C uses `pipe()` + `write(1)` to wake\n183\t  a `select(2)`-blocked loop) → `std::sync::mpsc`,\n184\t  `std::sync::Condvar`, or a self-pipe via `nix`.  Real bodies.\n185\t* **Event loops (`picoquic_packet_loop_v3` etc.)** are the heart\n186\t  of the library.  Translate the receive-process-send loop\n187\t  directly.\n188\t* **`select(2)` / `poll(2)` / `io_uring`** → use the `mio` crate\n189\t  (or `tokio` lower-level bits) for portable poll, or\n190\t  platform-specific `nix` bindings.  Real Rust code.\n191\t* **Loglib functions (binlog / qlog / textlog / performance_log\n192\t  / logger).**  Tests call them; translate them.  Use the\n193\t  `log` crate plus standard file I/O.\n194\t* **Sub-system not yet translated** is never a valid blocker.\n195\t  Translate the sub-system **first**, then come back.  The whole\n196\t  library has to be translated; do it in dependency order.\n197\t\n198\t### What to do instead when a body is hard\n199\t\n200\t1. **Translate the helpers it needs first.**  A function that\n201\t   \"needs `picoquic_get_stored_ticket`\" really means \"translate\n202\t   `picoquic_get_stored_ticket` first.\"  That helper is the\n203\t   blocker; resolve it.\n204\t2. **Add the missing fields / types.**  If a C body reads\n205\t   `cnx->nb_zero_rtt_sent` and the Rust struct doesn't have it,\n206\t   **add it** (it's already in `internal.rs` as\n207\t   `Connection::nb_zero_rtt_sent` — verify before assuming\n208\t   absence).\n209\t3. **Use Rust ownership idioms.**  \"Returns `&mut` from `&self`\"\n210\t   means the signature wants `&mut self` or `RefCell`, not\n211\t   \"return `None`.\"\n212\t4. **Port C static tables to Rust `static`/`const`.**  If a\n213\t   version-table or cipher-suite array is \"not yet wired up,\"\n214\t   wire it up.\n215\t5. **Extend Phase 1/2 traits when a body needs it.**  If `Socket`\n216\t   doesn't expose `open_with_options` and a body needs it, add\n217\t   the trait method.  The shape is mutable until tests pass.\n218\t\n219\t### When `todo!()` is the only option\n220\t\n221\tAlmost never.  The only acceptable unfinished state is a single\n222\tbare `todo!()` with a one-line **real-blocker** note above it.\n223\t\"Real blocker\" means a concrete external dependency you cannot\n224\tresolve in this pass, e.g.:\n225\t\n226\t* \"blocked: needs picotls API not yet exposed in the picotls\n227\t  Rust binding\"\n228\t* \"blocked: needs OS-level kernel-tls socket option that doesn't\n229\t  exist on this platform\"\n230\t\n231\tThese are **NOT** valid blockers — every one is a shortcut:\n232\t\n233\t* \"deferred to a future pass\" / \"follow-on work\"\n234\t* \"out of scope\"\n235\t* \"multi-threading not in scope\" (use `std::thread`)\n236\t* \"loglib not in scope\" (translate the helpers)\n237\t* \"sub-system not yet translated\" (translate the sub-system FIRST)\n238\t* \"needs more design thought\"\n239\t* \"borrow-checker issue\" (use `&mut self` / `RefCell` /\n240\t  restructure)\n241\t* \"lifetime issue\" (clone, restructure, or change the signature\n242\t  if Phase 1 got it wrong)\n243\t\n244\tTranslate the function.\n245\t\n246\t---\n247\t\n248\t## The \"tests are the gate\" workflow\n249\t\n250\tFor each translated function:\n251\t\n252\t1. **Find the tests that exercise it.**  `grep -lE \"<fn name>\" rs/fq/src/tests/`\n253\t   often points right at them.  The Phase 3 known-answer tests\n254\t   (intformat, varint, siphash, splay, hashtest, code_version)\n255\t   exercise data-structure leaves first.\n256\t2. **Translate the body.**  Use Edit; preserve the existing\n257\t   signature and doc comment.  Don't rename / reshape.\n258\t3. **Run `cargo test <test_name>`.**  If it still panics, the\n259\t   panic backtrace tells you which other `todo!()` is in the\n260\t   way.  Translate that next, then rerun.\n261\t4. **Repeat until the test passes.**  Then move to the next test.\n262\t\n263\tBottom-up order is the natural one.  In the Phase 0 call graph,\n264\tfunction-level \"max height\" gives the depth.  Leaves first means\n265\tdata-structure tests light up first; connection-lifecycle tests\n266\tlast.\n267\t\n268\t---\n269\t\n270\t## When to add new types\n271\t\n272\t* The C type catalog is in [`crate::internal`] (private structs\n273\t  internal to the library) and [`crate::lib`] (public types\n274\t  re-exported at the crate root).  These are stable; don't add\n275\t  fields unless absolutely necessary.\n276\t* If the C body needs a `static` table (e.g. cipher-suite\n277\t  registries, frame-name tables, per-version parameter blocks),\n278\t  put it in the same module as the function that consumes it,\n279\t  as a `pub(crate) const` or `static`.\n280\t* Helpers that are only used by one function: nest with `fn`\n281\t  inside that function or as a private `fn` immediately above.\n282\t  Keep the public surface lean.\n283\t\n284\t---\n285\t\n286\t## Conservative refactors allowed\n287\t\n288\tThe Phase 4 prompt forbids \"rename / reshape\" of existing\n289\titems.  But these refactors are explicitly allowed:\n290\t\n291\t* **Splitting a long fn into helpers** when the C body has a\n292\t  natural segmentation (e.g. parse → validate → record).  Keep\n293\t  the public surface — the helpers are private.\n294\t* **Replacing a manual loop with an iterator chain** when the\n295\t  semantics are exactly preserved.\n296\t* **Replacing a manual `for` with `.copy_from_slice`,\n297\t  `.iter().sum()`, `.windows(N)`, etc.**\n298\t* **Changing local variable types** (e.g. `usize` → `u32` if the\n299\t  range fits) when the body's invariants justify it.\n300\t\n301\tIf a refactor crosses a public-API boundary (changing a\n302\tparameter type, adding a return value, splitting one fn into\n303\ttwo public ones), surface it on stdout and stop — that's a\n304\thuman design call.\n305\t\n306\t---\n307\t\n308\t## Module map (where to translate what)\n309\t\n310\t| Rust module | C source | Notes |\n311\t|---|---|---|\n312\t| `crate::siphash` | `picoquic/picohash.c` (siphash bit) | Known-answer tested.  Pure leaf. |\n313\t| `crate::splay` | `picoquic/picosplay.c` | Token-based splay tree; tests cover insert/find/delete/iter. |\n314\t| `crate::hash` | `picoquic/picohash.c` | Hash table over arena tokens. |\n315\t| `crate::arena` | (Phase 2 design — no C counterpart) | Slotmap. |\n316\t| `crate::bytestream` | `picoquic/bytestream.c` | Cursor over `&mut [u8]` plus owned-buffer helper. |\n317\t| `crate::utils` | `picoquic/picoquic_utils.c` | uint8_to_str, constant_time_memcmp, addr helpers. |\n318\t| `crate::errors` | `picoquic/picoquic_internal.h` consts | `name()` lookup tables for the two enum sets. |\n319\t| `crate::frames::FrameType::name` | `picoquic/frames.c::picoquic_frame_name` | Lookup table. |\n320\t| `crate::tp::TransportParameter::name` | `picoquic/transport.c::picoquic_tp_name` | Lookup table. |\n321\t| `crate::internal::{parse,format}_*` | `picoquic/picoquic_utils.c` | One-line bodies; mostly already translated. |\n322\t| `crate::internal::varint_*` | `picoquic/frames.c` | RFC 9000 §16. |\n323\t| `crate::internal::get_packet_number64` | `picoquic/packet.c` | RFC 9000 §A.3 reconstruction. |\n324\t| `crate::header_protection` | `picoquic/protoop_aead.c` (header bits) | RFC 9001 §5.4. |\n325\t| `crate::sys::picotls` / `::openssl` | picotls / openssl bindings | Phase 4 wires real crypto. |\n326\t| `crate::tls_api::*` | `picoquic/tls_api.c` | Master TLS context, retry tokens, app-secret rotation. |\n327\t| `crate::lb::*` | `picoquic/cid_for_lb.c` | LB CID encryption. |\n328\t| `crate::config::*` | `picoquic/quic_config.c` (sample-app uses) | Command-line option parsing. |\n329\t| `crate::binlog`, `::logger`, `::qlog`, `::textlog` | `loglib/*` | (out of v1 scope per plan; if any todo!() left, leave them) |\n330\t| `crate::packet_loop` | `picoquic/sockloop.c` | Network event loop. |\n331\t| `crate::socks*` | `picoquic/picosocks.c` | UDP socket abstraction. |\n332\t| `crate::internal::Quic` / `Connection` / `Path` impls | `picoquic/quicctx.c`, `cnx_*.c`, `paths.c`, … | The bulk: ~200 fns each on Quic / Connection. |\n333\t\n334\tRecommended order (bottom-up): siphash, splay, hash, arena (no\n335\ttodo!()s left there?), bytestream, utils, errors, frames, tp,\n336\tvarint helpers, get_packet_number64, header_protection, then up\n337\tto lb, config, tls_api, then Connection / Path / Quic core, then\n338\tsys backends, then network event loop / socks.\n339\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01T3AJCWTSoTU1NtzVKtEs23","session_id":"ba987d13-6cc7-465f-bc67-aa9a57ac7370","uuid":"c27d0dac-0f67-43a8-9e6c-c4f69992241f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T12:45:15.781Z"}
